    "lawCode": "Amd §363-a, Soc Serv L; amd §8, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6716\n\nSPONSOR: WEBER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law and the state finance law, in\nrelation to requiring enhanced federal medical assistance percentage\nfunds be directly shared with the counties and the city of New York\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nRequires that enhanced federal medical assistance percentage funds be\ndirectly shared with the counties and New York City.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 - Amends subdivision 4 of section 363-a of the social services\nlaw, as amended by chapter 474 of the laws of 1996 by adding the\nlanguage: Any and all grants of the enhanced federal medical assistance\npercentage funds awarded to the state shall be directly distributed to\nthe counties and the city of New York proportionate to the county and\ncity of New York share of the non-federal share of medical assistance.\n\nSection 2 - Provides for a thorough examination, audit, and identifica-\ntion of all funds received from the enhanced federal medical assistance\npercentage and the distributions made to counties.\n\nSection 3 - Sets effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCongress delivers federal funds directly to states to cover a share of\ntheir Medicaid programs. Congress intended these federal funds, known as\neFMAP, to be shared with counties and New York City. Under Governor\nHochul's 2024 budget, the Governor absorbs up to $1 billion eFMAP funds\ninto the state, putting county-administered Medicaid services on the\nchopping block.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
